--TEST--
An uncaught throw drains the `Core\Script::onExit` queue with the live `Throwable` on the report, at status 1 — `rule:observability/three-endings-fire-the-exit-queue` and `rule:observability/exit-hooks-run-after-the-ladder-before-teardown`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::reason
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::error
// The third ending, and the only one whose report carries an error. What the
// hook is handed is the object the program threw — not a record built from it
// — so its own `message` reads back through the ordinary property.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    string $reason = match ($report->reason()) {
        Core\Script\ExitReason::Normal => "Normal",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::ExitCall => "ExitCall",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::UncaughtThrow => "UncaughtThrow",
    };
    echo "onExit: ", $reason, " ", $report->status(), "\n";
    ?Throwable $error = $report->error();
    if ($error != null) {
        echo "error: ", $error->message, "\n";
    }
});

// The hooks run after `rule:errors/escalation-ladder`'s ladder has already reported the failure —
// the failure hooks come first so that a misbehaving queue cannot starve the
// failure report — and the `finally` below runs before either, because an
// unwind is what carries the throw to the root in the first place.
try {
    echo "about to fail\n";
    throw new RuntimeError("the store said no");
} finally {
    echo "the finally ran\n";
}
--EXPECT--
about to fail
the finally ran
onExit: UncaughtThrow 1
error: the store said no
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
%A
